  "Question": "For the nonzero numbers \\(a,\\) \\(b,\\) and \\(c,\\) define \\[(a,b,c)=\\dfrac{a}{b}+\\dfrac{b}{c}+\\dfrac{c}{a}.\\] Find \\((2,12,9).\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\)",
    "B": "\\(5\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\)",
    "D": "\\(7\\)",
    "E": "\\(8\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The definition tells us to substitute \\(a=2\\), \\(b=12\\), and \\(c=9\\), in that order. These numbers are all nonzero, so every fraction is defined. Simplifying and using the common denominator \\(6\\), we get\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2,12,9)&=\\frac{2}{12}+\\frac{12}{9}+\\frac{9}{2}\\\\\n&=\\frac16+\\frac43+\\frac92\\\\\n&=\\frac{1+8+27}{6}=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{6}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
